s conjectured to be cut-off.\n\nConsider random walk on group \\(SL_n(\\mathbb{F}_q)\\) with some small set of generators. The following is conjectured:\n\n**Conjecture 2.** It is known that this random walk has spectral gap bounded away from 0 for all \\(n\\), that is, \\(SL_n(\\mathbb{F}_q)\\) is expander. It is conjectured that there is cutoff.\n\n4. SPEAKER IV: BEN MORRIS\n\nIt is known that Thorp shuffling has mixing time \\(O(poly(\\log n))\\) for deck of \\(n\\) cards when \\(n=2^d\\) for some \\(d\\in\\mathbb{N}\\) (See work by Morris). The following is conjectured:\n\n**Open Question 5.** For any \\(n\\), the mixing time is \\(O(poly(\\log n))\\). Further, it is \\(\\Theta(\\log^2 n)\\).\n\n5. SPEAKER V: DAVID ALDOUS\n\n(Notes contributed by Aldous)\n\nConsider a finite state irreducible continuous-time Markov chain. Write \\(\\pi\\) for its stationary distribution. For each initial state \\(x\\) there is a first time \\(\\tau_x\\) such that the variation distance between \\(P_x(X_t\\in\\cdot)\\) and \\(\\pi(\\cdot)\\) is at most \\(1/4\\), say. Define \\(\\tau\\) as the median of \\(\\tau_x\\) when \\(x\\) is distributed as \\(\\pi\\). Define \\(\\tau_2\\) as the relaxation time (= \\(1/(\\)spectral gap\\()\\)) of the chain. Here is a slight modification of Conjecture 1 of Yuval Peres.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProbabilistic combinatorics at exponentially small scales - arXiv Combinatorics (https://arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2512.15077v1)\nciteturn2search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9 months ago; Crawled: last week; ## Probabilistic combinatorics at exponentially small scales ... # • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n## Probabilistic combinatorics at exponentially small scales\n\nJulian Sahasrabudhe\n\nPublished: 2025-12-17\n\nComments: Survey for the 2026 ICM\n\nNote:\n\nDOI: 10.1137/25m1826755\n\nprobabilistic combinatorics\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nIn many applications of the probabilistic method, one looks to study phenomena that occur ``with high probability''. More recently however, in an attempt to understand some of the most fundamental problems in combinatorics, researchers have been diving deeper into these probability spaces and understanding phenomena that occur at much smaller probability scales. Here I will survey a few of these ideas from the perspective of my own work in the area.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe singularity probability conjecture for random symmetric Bernoulli matrices | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/320971/the-singularity-probability-conjecture-for-random-symmetric)\nciteturn2search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; # The singularity probability conjecture for random symmetric Bernoulli matrices ... Let $M_n$M n​ be a random symmetric $n$n by $n$n matrix whose upper-diagonal entries are iid Bernoulli random variables taking values $\\pm1$±1 with probability $1/2$1/2, and let $q_n$q n​ be the probability that $M_n$M n​ is singular.\n\n# The singularity probability conjecture for random symmetric Bernoulli matrices\n\nAbout 15 years old · traced to 2011\n\nLet $M_n$M n​ be a random symmetric $n$n by $n$n matrix whose upper-diagonal entries are iid Bernoulli random variables taking values $\\pm1$±1 with probability $1/2$1/2, and let $q_n$q n​ be the probability that $M_n$M n​ is singular.\n\nSymmetric-matrix singularity conjecture. The singularity probability satisfies\n\n$$q_n=\\left(\\frac{1}{2}+o(1)\\right)^n.$$q n​=(2 1​+o(1))n.\n\nThis conjecture predicts exponential decay for the singularity probability of random symmetric Bernoulli matrices. The paper proves decay faster than every polynomial, while exponential decay of the conjectured scale remained open.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nHoi H. Nguyen, “Inverse Littlewood-Offord problems and The Singularity of Random Symmetric Matrices”, arXiv:1101.3074 (2012).\n\nprobabilityrandom-matricessingularity\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEuropean Congress of Mathematics. Portorož, 20–26 June 2021 (https://content.preview.ems.press/assets/public/full-texts/books/262/online-pdf/978-3-98547-551-3.pdf)\nciteturn2search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.2 years ago; In a breakthrough paper, Tikhomirov [64] (see also [54]) could exactly estimate it, by showing that the best strategy to achieve singularity is to have a zero column or row vector. ... Such an optimal estimate is not yet known for the symmetric Bernoulli matrix \\(B_n\\) (even though it is conjectured) but the paper [32] proves that the probability that it is singular is bounded above by \\(e^{-O(\\sqrt{n})}\\).\n\n**4.4. The singularity probability**\n\nA well-known problem has been to estimate the probability that a matrix \\(\\widetilde{B}_n\\) with all independent Bernoulli entries (hence not self-adjoint) is singular. In a breakthrough paper, Tikhomirov [64] (see also [54]) could exactly estimate it, by showing that the best strategy to achieve singularity is to have a zero column or row vector.\n\n**Theorem 4.8.** *There exists a finite constant \\(C\\) such that if \\(C\\ln n/n\\le p\\le \\frac12\\),*\n\n\\[\n\\mathbb{P}(\\widetilde{B}_n\\ \\text{is singular})=(2+o_n(1))(1-p)^n n.\n\\]\n\nSuch an optimal estimate is not yet known for the symmetric Bernoulli matrix \\(B_n\\) (even though it is conjectured) but the paper [32] proves that the probability that it is singular is bounded above by \\(e^{-O(\\sqrt{n})}\\). This was improved in an exponential upper bound in [31].\n\n**5. Open problems**\n\n(1) Local law for Bernoulli matrices when \\(pn\\) is 